          "text": "{\"schema_version\":\"ai-evaluator-v2/1.1\",\"case_id\":\"6-3\",\"candidate_id\":\"candidate_3\",\"action_assessment\":{\"status\":\"defect\",\"summary\":\"The graph captures determining requirements, PPS entry, missing-parts procurement, and implementation, but it omits the required manual transfer of data to the ERP system and distorts the final quality-check activity into a recheck/retry construct.\"},\"flow_assessment\":{\"status\":\"uncertain\",\"summary\":\"The preserved matched actions follow the expected overall order, but the omitted ERP-transfer step makes the required sequencing around that source step not fully assessable.\"},\"control_flow_assessment\":{\"status\":\"defect\",\"summary\":\"The missing-parts decision with an optional procurement branch is preserved, but the ending is changed into an unsupported success/retry loop that sends the process back from quality control to implementation.\"},\"main_error_type\":\"mixed_or_cascading\",\"explanation\":\"The source requires: determine parts/quantities/date, enter data into PPS, manually transfer the data to ERP as a second step, then decide whether procurement is needed, proceed to implementation when production is to start, and finally perform a quality check. The generated graph preserves the main preparation and procurement-decision skeleton, but it drops the manual ERP transfer entirely and replaces the simple final quality check with a decision-based retry loop that can return to implementation. These changes materially alter both process completeness and the end-of-process behavior, though much of the core sequence remains recognizable.\",\"overall_score\":0.63,\"severity\":\"moderate\",\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"ambiguity_flag\":false,\"ambiguity_explanation\":\"\",\"requires_human_review\":false,\"review_reason\":\"\"}"
